# linkcheck: destination health checker

`linkcheck/` verifies that the destinations behind short links still respond and classifies every
result. It is a standalone library (standard library only): it needs no running server, and every
network and DNS access is injectable, so it is fully unit-testable without network access.

## Usage

```python
from linkcheck import LinkChecker, summarize

results = LinkChecker().check_many(["https://example.org/", "http://127.0.0.1/admin"])
print(summarize(results).render())
```

`LinkChecker.check(url)` returns a `CheckResult` (`url`, `classification`, `status_code`,
`attempts`, `reason`, `location`) and never raises for a bad URL or an unreachable host.
`summarize(results)` returns a `Report` with the number of links per classification and the list
of unhealthy links (`render()` for text, `to_dict()` for JSON).

## Classification

| classification | meaning |
|---|---|
| `healthy` | 2xx response |
| `redirecting` | 3xx response; the `Location` is reported and never followed |
| `client_error` | 4xx response, or a host name that does not resolve |
| `server_error` | 5xx response, or a transport failure (refused, reset, TLS failure) |
| `timeout` | the time budget of an attempt ran out |
| `blocked` | refused by the safety policy (non-http(s) scheme, private destination, bad URL) |

`client_error`, `server_error`, `timeout` and `blocked` are *unhealthy*. `redirecting` is counted
but not listed as unhealthy: the destination still answers (an assumption of this module).

## Time budget and retries

* One attempt has a **total** deadline of **@@TIMEOUT_SECONDS@@ seconds** (connect, TLS and
  response head), not a per-read timeout. Valid range: 1 to 60 seconds.
* Timeouts and 5xx responses are **transient** and retried up to **@@MAX_RETRIES@@ times**
  (range 0 to 5) with exponential back-off: @@BACKOFF_SCHEDULE@@. Every other outcome is final.
* Configure with `Settings(timeout_seconds=..., max_retries=..., backoff_base_seconds=...)`;
  values outside the ranges raise `ValueError`.

## Safety policy

* Only `http` and `https` are accepted; credentials in a URL are refused.
* A destination that is, or resolves to, a loopback, private, link-local, shared (CGNAT),
  reserved, multicast or unspecified address is `blocked` and never contacted. IP literals are
  recognised in every spelling (dotted, decimal, hex, octal, short forms, IPv6, IPv4-mapped IPv6).
  A name is blocked when ANY of its DNS answers is non-public.
* The vetted addresses are pinned: the fetcher connects to exactly those and never resolves the
  name again (no DNS rebinding). HTTPS verifies the certificate against the original host name.
* Redirects are never followed, proxy environment variables are ignored and no cookies or
  credentials are sent. The response body is never read.
* Untrusted text in reports (URLs, reasons) is sanitised so it cannot forge report lines.

## Testing offline

`LinkChecker(fetcher, settings=..., resolver=..., sleep=...)` takes every side effect as an
argument: a `Fetcher` (one method, `fetch(FetchRequest) -> FetchResponse`), a resolver
(`host -> [addresses]`) and a `sleep` function. `tests/linkcheck/` uses fakes for all three.

